{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BTSDoC5XoSWDpX9LB7Fdeb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd026-cut\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/cut: Remove Sections from Lines\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  macOS BSD cut lacks the --complement and --output-delimiter flags present in GNU cut.\n6\t  Scripts that invert field selections or use a different output delimiter depend on GNU\n7\t  behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gcut,\n8\t  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential\n9\t  testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\tgoals:\n12\t  - G1: Extract selected byte ranges, character ranges, or delimited fields from each line of input, matching GNU cut behavior.\n13\t  - G2: Support -b (bytes), -c (characters), and -f (fields) selection modes with range lists.\n14\t  - G3: Support -d to configure the field delimiter and --output-delimiter to control the separator in the output.\n15\t  - G4: Support --complement to invert the selection (output what was not selected).\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Byte and character selection (-b, -c)\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: -b LIST must extract the specified byte positions from each line. LIST is a comma-separated list of ranges; each range is N, N-M, N- (from N to end), or -M (from start to M). Bytes are 1-indexed.\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: -c LIST must extract the specified character positions. Under LC_ALL=C, -c and -b are equivalent (one byte per character). The flag is provided for compatibility.\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: With -b or -c, newline characters are not counted as part of the line; they are passed through to terminate each output line.\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: Lines shorter than the selected range produce no bytes for the out-of-range positions; only the bytes that exist are output.\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Field selection (-f, -d, -s)\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: -f LIST must extract the specified fields from each line, where fields are delimited by the character given with -d (default tab). LIST uses the same range syntax as -b.\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: -d DELIM must set the input field delimiter to the single character DELIM. The delimiter must be exactly one byte. When the output delimiter is not explicitly set with --output-delimiter, the input delimiter is also used as the output delimiter.\n37\t      - R2.3:\n38\t          text: -s must suppress lines that do not contain the delimiter. Without -s, lines with no delimiter are printed unchanged.\n39\t      - R2.4:\n40\t          text: --output-delimiter STRING must use STRING as the separator between selected output fields, replacing the default (which is the input delimiter).\n41\t\n42\t  R3:\n43\t    title: Complement mode (--complement)\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R3.1:\n46\t          text: --complement must invert the selection; the output contains the bytes, characters, or fields NOT specified by the range list.\n47\t      - R3.2:\n48\t          text: --complement is compatible with -b, -c, and -f. It is not compatible with -s.\n49\t      - R3.3:\n50\t          text: With --complement and -f, fields not in the list are output in their original order, separated by the output delimiter.\n51\t\n52\t  R4:\n53\t    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n54\t    items:\n55\t      - R4.1:\n56\t          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n57\t      - R4.2:\n58\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n59\t      - R4.3:\n60\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n61\t      - R4.4:\n62\t          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n63\t\n64\tnon_goals:\n65\t  - cmd/cut does not support multi-byte delimiters; -d accepts exactly one byte.\n66\t  - cmd/cut does not implement locale-aware multi-byte character cutting for -c; LC_ALL=C is assumed.\n67\t\n68\tacceptance_criteria:\n69\t  - id: AC1\n70\t    criterion: \"printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 produces 'b\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f2.\"\n71\t    traces:\n72\t      - R2.1\n73\t      - R2.2\n74\t  - id: AC2\n75\t    criterion: \"printf 'abcdef\\\\n' | cut -b2-4 produces 'bcd\\\\n', matching gcut -b2-4.\"\n76\t    traces:\n77\t      - R1.1\n78\t      - R1.2\n79\t      - R1.3\n80\t      - R1.4\n81\t  - id: AC3\n82\t    criterion: \"printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f1,3 produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f1,3.\"\n83\t    traces:\n84\t      - R2.1\n85\t      - R2.2\n86\t  - id: AC4\n87\t    criterion: \"printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 --complement produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut --complement.\"\n88\t    traces:\n89\t      - R2.1\n90\t      - R2.2\n91\t      - R3.1\n92\t      - R3.2\n93\t      - R3.3\n94\t  - id: AC5\n95\t    criterion: \"printf 'no-delimiter\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 -s produces empty output, matching gcut -s.\"\n96\t    traces:\n97\t      - R2.1\n98\t      - R2.2\n99\t      - R2.3\n100\t      - R3.2\n101\t  - id: AC6\n102\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gcut passes for all required test cases.\"\n103\t    traces:\n104\t      - R1.1\n105\t      - R1.2\n106\t      - R1.3\n107\t      - R1.4\n108\t  - id: AC7\n109\t    criterion: \"cmd/cut compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n110\t    traces:\n111\t      - R1.1\n112\t      - R1.2\n113\t      - R1.3\n114\t      - R1.4\n115\t      - R2.1\n116\t      - R2.2\n117\t      - R2.3\n118\t      - R2.4\n119\t      - R3.1\n120\t      - R3.2\n121\t      - R3.3\n122\t      - R4.1\n123\t      - R4.2\n124\t      - R4.3\n125\t      - R4.4\n126\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e153b82c-89ca-4b8b-a858-047942f1f01d","uuid":"09dafbfd-3e05-4c85-9b42-8cc355ce6fe8","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:51:10.258Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5278/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd026-cut.yaml","content":"id: srd026-cut\ntitle: \"cmd/cut: Remove Sections from Lines\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD cut lacks the --complement and --output-delimiter flags present in GNU cut.\n  Scripts that invert field selections or use a different output delimiter depend on GNU\n  behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gcut,\n  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential\n  testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Extract selected byte ranges, character ranges, or delimited fields from each line of input, matching GNU cut behavior.\n  - G2: Support -b (bytes), -c (characters), and -f (fields) selection modes with range lists.\n  - G3: Support -d to configure the field delimiter and --output-delimiter to control the separator in the output.\n  - G4: Support --complement to invert the selection (output what was not selected).\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Byte and character selection (-b, -c)\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: -b LIST must extract the specified byte positions from each line. LIST is a comma-separated list of ranges; each range is N, N-M, N- (from N to end), or -M (from start to M). Bytes are 1-indexed.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: -c LIST must extract the specified character positions. Under LC_ALL=C, -c and -b are equivalent (one byte per character). The flag is provided for compatibility.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: With -b or -c, newline characters are not counted as part of the line; they are passed through to terminate each output line.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Lines shorter than the selected range produce no bytes for the out-of-range positions; only the bytes that exist are output.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Field selection (-f, -d, -s)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: -f LIST must extract the specified fields from each line, where fields are delimited by the character given with -d (default tab). LIST uses the same range syntax as -b.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: -d DELIM must set the input field delimiter to the single character DELIM. The delimiter must be exactly one byte. When the output delimiter is not explicitly set with --output-delimiter, the input delimiter is also used as the output delimiter.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: -s must suppress lines that do not contain the delimiter. Without -s, lines with no delimiter are printed unchanged.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: --output-delimiter STRING must use STRING as the separator between selected output fields, replacing the default (which is the input delimiter).\n\n  R3:\n    title: Complement mode (--complement)\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: --complement must invert the selection; the output contains the bytes, characters, or fields NOT specified by the range list.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: --complement is compatible with -b, -c, and -f. It is not compatible with -s.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: With --complement and -f, fields not in the list are output in their original order, separated by the output delimiter.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R4.4:\n          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/cut does not support multi-byte delimiters; -d accepts exactly one byte.\n  - cmd/cut does not implement locale-aware multi-byte character cutting for -c; LC_ALL=C is assumed.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 produces 'b\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f2.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"printf 'abcdef\\\\n' | cut -b2-4 produces 'bcd\\\\n', matching gcut -b2-4.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f1,3 produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f1,3.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 --complement produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut --complement.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"printf 'no-delimiter\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 -s produces empty output, matching gcut -s.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.2\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gcut passes for all required test cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC7\n    criterion: \"cmd/cut compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n","numLines":126,"startLine":1,"totalLines":126}}}
